WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.867 --> 00:00:02.535
[music playing]

00:00:06.139 --> 00:00:07.507
NEIL DEGRASSE
TYSON: It used to be

00:00:07.574 --> 00:00:10.944
hard to keep food from
spoiling in the summertime.

00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:13.613
There was a person
called the ice man.

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:18.218
He would come to your house and
sell you a big block of ice.

00:00:18.284 --> 00:00:19.886
You'd keep it in
something called

00:00:19.953 --> 00:00:22.322
an ice box, to
preserve the kinds

00:00:22.389 --> 00:00:25.158
of food that spoil quickly.

00:00:25.225 --> 00:00:29.095
But that was a drag because
the ice kept melting.

00:00:29.162 --> 00:00:31.364
It would drip all
over the floor.

00:00:31.431 --> 00:00:35.235
So somebody thought up
another way to keep food cold.

00:00:35.301 --> 00:00:38.271
It was a gas-powered
system that used ammonia

00:00:38.338 --> 00:00:41.274
or sulfur dioxide as a coolant.

00:00:41.341 --> 00:00:44.077
No more lugging blocks of ice.

00:00:44.144 --> 00:00:46.579
What could be bad about that?

00:00:46.646 --> 00:00:48.982
The chemicals were
not only poisonous,

00:00:49.048 --> 00:00:50.984
they smelled terrible.

00:00:51.050 --> 00:00:51.851
And there were leaks.

00:00:54.454 --> 00:00:56.956
A substitute coolant
was badly needed.

00:00:57.023 --> 00:01:00.427
One that would circulate inside
the refrigerator, but would not

00:01:00.493 --> 00:01:03.096
poison anyone if the
refrigerator leaked.

00:01:03.163 --> 00:01:05.565
Or pose a danger if it
was sent to the junkyard.

00:01:08.101 --> 00:01:10.203
Something that
wouldn't make you sick.

00:01:10.270 --> 00:01:13.139
Wouldn't burn your
eyes or attract bugs.

00:01:13.206 --> 00:01:15.708
Or even bother the cat.

00:01:15.775 --> 00:01:21.448
But in all of nature, no such
material seemed to exist.

00:01:21.514 --> 00:01:25.718
So chemists in the United States
invented a class of molecules.

00:01:25.785 --> 00:01:28.188
Little collections
of even tinier things

00:01:28.254 --> 00:01:33.092
called atoms that had never
existed on Earth before.

00:01:33.159 --> 00:01:37.597
They call them
chlorofluorocarbons, or CFCs,

00:01:37.664 --> 00:01:40.934
Because they were made up of one
or more carbon atoms and some

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:44.604
chlorine and/or fluorine atoms.

00:01:44.671 --> 00:01:47.841
These new molecules
were wildly successful.

00:01:47.907 --> 00:01:50.910
Far exceeding the expectations
of their inventors.

00:02:05.058 --> 00:02:09.128
Not only did CFCs become the
chief coolant in refrigerators,

00:02:09.195 --> 00:02:11.798
but also in air conditioners.

00:02:11.865 --> 00:02:15.802
There were so many things
you could do with CFCs.

00:02:15.869 --> 00:02:22.108
People used them to propel great
fluffy mounds of shaving cream.

00:02:22.175 --> 00:02:27.180
And to protect your
hair from wind and rain.

00:02:27.247 --> 00:02:28.915
It was also the
propellant that made

00:02:28.982 --> 00:02:33.853
fire extinguishers and spray
paint cans so much fun.

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:37.323
It was good for foam
insulation, industrial solvents,

00:02:37.390 --> 00:02:39.792
and cleansing agents.

00:02:39.859 --> 00:02:42.328
The most famous brand
name of these chemicals

00:02:42.395 --> 00:02:46.466
was freon, a
trademark of DuPont.

00:02:46.533 --> 00:02:49.402
It was used for
decades and no harm

00:02:49.469 --> 00:02:51.571
ever seemed to come from it.

00:02:51.638 --> 00:02:55.708
Safe as safe could
be, everyone figured.

00:02:55.775 --> 00:02:59.712
Until in the early 1970s,
two atmospheric chemists

00:02:59.779 --> 00:03:02.081
at the University
of California Irvine

00:03:02.148 --> 00:03:05.084
were studying
Earth's atmosphere.

00:03:05.151 --> 00:03:08.321
Mario Molina was a
Mexican immigrant,

00:03:08.388 --> 00:03:10.189
a young laser chemist.

00:03:10.256 --> 00:03:13.660
Sherwood Rowland was a
chemical kineticist--

00:03:13.726 --> 00:03:16.996
someone who studied the
motions of molecules and gases

00:03:17.063 --> 00:03:19.165
under varying conditions.

00:03:19.232 --> 00:03:21.935
He was from a
small town in Ohio.

00:03:22.001 --> 00:03:25.271
Molina wanted to
grow as a scientist.

00:03:25.338 --> 00:03:26.739
He was looking
for a project that

00:03:26.806 --> 00:03:29.175
would take him as far
from his previous research

00:03:29.242 --> 00:03:31.244
experience as possible.

00:03:31.311 --> 00:03:35.181
He wondered, what happens
to those freon molecules

00:03:35.248 --> 00:03:37.650
when they leak out of
the air conditioner?

00:03:37.717 --> 00:03:40.386
This was a time when
the Apollo astronauts

00:03:40.453 --> 00:03:43.990
were still making regularly
scheduled trips to the moon.

00:03:44.057 --> 00:03:47.060
And NASA was contemplating
weekly launches

00:03:47.126 --> 00:03:49.362
of a space shuttle.

00:03:49.429 --> 00:03:52.098
Would all that burning
rocket fuel pose

00:03:52.165 --> 00:03:54.601
a danger to the stratosphere--

00:03:54.667 --> 00:03:57.003
that place where
Earth's atmosphere

00:03:57.070 --> 00:04:00.139
meets the blackness of space?

00:04:00.206 --> 00:04:02.675
And this is how science
works a lot of the time.

00:04:02.742 --> 00:04:05.144
You set out to solve
one problem and you

00:04:05.211 --> 00:04:07.180
happen on a
completely different,

00:04:07.246 --> 00:04:08.781
unexpected phenomena.

00:04:11.517 --> 00:04:14.754
Those wonderfully
inert, harmless CFCs,

00:04:14.821 --> 00:04:18.825
the magic molecules of
shaving cream and hairspray,

00:04:18.891 --> 00:04:22.262
didn't simply vanish when
we were done with them.

00:04:22.328 --> 00:04:25.398
They had an afterlife
at the edge of space,

00:04:25.465 --> 00:04:28.534
where they accumulated
in the trillions.

00:04:28.601 --> 00:04:32.705
They were silently congregating
high above the Earth

00:04:32.772 --> 00:04:34.273
and they were up to no good.

00:04:36.776 --> 00:04:41.381
Molina and Roland were alarmed
to discover that the CFCs had

00:04:41.447 --> 00:04:44.917
thinned the protective layer
that shielded us from the sun's

00:04:44.984 --> 00:04:47.153
harmful ultraviolet radiation.

00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:51.124
And it was getting
worse all the time.

00:04:51.190 --> 00:04:54.460
When UV light hits
a CFC molecule,

00:04:54.527 --> 00:04:57.463
it strips away the
chlorine atoms.

00:04:57.530 --> 00:05:01.167
Once that happens, the
chlorine atoms start devouring

00:05:01.234 --> 00:05:03.770
the precious ozone molecules.

00:05:03.836 --> 00:05:06.939
It wasn't until our planet
developed an ozone layer,

00:05:07.006 --> 00:05:09.308
about two and a half
billion years ago,

00:05:09.375 --> 00:05:13.946
that it was safe for life to
leave the ocean for the land.

00:05:14.013 --> 00:05:16.783
A single chlorine
atom can destroy

00:05:16.849 --> 00:05:17.850
100,000 ozone molecules.

